@meta{
  id: cli.check-links
}

`sisho check-links [PATH]`: check that the links in documents to other files and documents are not broken.

Status: implemented, apart from the points under "Where it differs from what it should be" at the end. It was `tomet check-links` and moved here: it follows a whole vault and keeps a cache outside it, which is the ecosystem's business and not one document's.

=[ What it checks ]

For each kind of link, whether the target exists in the vault.

@table
|[ Kind                     ][ Broken means ]
|[ File (`file:`), embed    ][ the target does not exist as a file ]
|[ Directory (`dir:`)       ][ the target does not exist as a directory ]
|[ Document (`tmt:path#id`) ][ the target does not exist as a document; with an `#id`, that document has no such id ]
|[ Reference (`ref:name`)   ][ no file in the vault has that name, either as its file name or as its file name without the extension (case-sensitive) ]

Not checked: external URLs, and anchors inside the same document (`#id`, `id:`). `tomet check` checks anchors inside the same document.

"Exists" means exists in the vault. A file on disk that `.gitignore` or `workspace.ignore` leaves out of the vault is not a target.

=[ How a target is measured ]

- A target starting with `./` or `../` is measured from the directory of the file that holds the link.
- A target with neither is measured from the root of the vault.
- What follows the `#` of a document link is checked by reading that document, apart from whether the path exists.

=[ What it looks at ]

- If `PATH` is a document, that one. If it is a directory, every `.tmt` under it, counted through the vault's index. Without it, `.`. A path that does not exist is an error: the check could not be done.
- The config is found by walking up from `PATH`. Checking one document still decides whether a target exists against the whole vault.

=[ The cache ]

The links of each document are kept in an SQLite cache, looked up by the file's modification time. A document that has not changed is not read again.

- It lives in the user's cache directory (`~/.cache/tomet/` on Unix), never inside the vault.
- One database serves every vault. Rows of vaults that are gone are swept at every run.
- The result does not depend on whether there is a cache.

=[ Output ]

Standard output.

- By default, a first line `<n> file(s) scanned, <m> link(s) checked, <k> broken`, then for each broken link `  <file>:<line>:<column>: broken link -> <target>`. If the file can no longer be read, the position is `?:?`.
- A document that could not be read goes to standard error as `  <file>: <reason>`.
- `--json` gives the same as one JSON object: `files_scanned`, `links_checked`, `broken` (`source`, `target`, `line`, `column`) and `errors` (`source`, `error`).

When the reader of the output goes away early (`| head`), it ends quietly with 141, not with a panic.

=[ Exit code ]

1 if any link is broken. 1 if any document could not be read (nobody checked the links that may have been in it). 0 if neither. When the check itself could not be done (no such path, the cache cannot be opened), an error is printed and the exit code is 1.

=[ Where it differs from what it should be ]

- The module description of `tomet-links` says references match "regardless of case and extension"; in fact it is an exact match of the file name, or of the file name without the extension.
- Nothing in either repository's gate runs it today: `tomet` has 69 broken links and `twrit` 2 (2026-10-18), which are stale references to directories that no longer exist. It joins `sisho check` once they are cleaned up.

=[ Not decided ]

- What a target starting with `/` means. Today it is provisional.
- Whether to check external URLs.
